; A `0x` prefix with no hex digit after it is a parse diagnostic, not the
; number zero. Three shapes: the bare prefix, a leading `_` (grouping needs
; a digit to group), and a non-hex letter.
;
; The `.invalid` token spans only the prefix, so the tail lexes normally —
; `_F` and `GG` each become an ordinary symbol — and one typo yields one
; diagnostic instead of a cascade. Reading `0x` as zero-with-unit-`x` is the
; silent failure hex literals exist to remove, so keeping that reading for
; the degenerate case would have kept the bug for the typo that matters
; most.
;
; The path is empty for all three: a malformed top-level literal belongs to
; no form.
0x
0x_F
0xGG
